 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186406853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406876 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "2bf21969-de75-57e4-b536-3fbad5aa15ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406877 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406877 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406877 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406877 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406877 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406877 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406877 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406877 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186406866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406877 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "9a0b26bf-4452-55f8-96b7-34adf0df266d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406878 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406878 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406878 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406878 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406878 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406878 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406878 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406878 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186406872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406878 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c77beab8-8132-549d-89ed-5a3840170386" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406879 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406879 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406879 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406879 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406879 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186406868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406879 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406879 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406879 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186406867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406879 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dc08e474-6dee-51de-a67a-78ce180dd388" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406881 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406881 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94867" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406881 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406881 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}$\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406881 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "455cce4b-aebd-5209-87a1-ec6ed81f2e6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406881 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]=2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-4)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{21}=0:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+b\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & c \\\\ 0 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406882 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406882 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107533" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406882 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406882 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406882 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5b0d7b89-c3c8-5cee-b6a2-01b5cd1b1f67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406882 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{4}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}={x}_{4}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]=3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-4)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-2)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-5)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the set is linearly independent and for any matrix in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R),$ we get \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]=a\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-b)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+(-c)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+d\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406884 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186406887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406884 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "748c2276-dafd-5926-8dff-6ce428e10c5e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406884 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186406883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406885 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186406888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406885 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "aa1dd52